+*   **No Direct Rendering:** DCT does not support direct rendering from the compression domain; it requires on-demand decompression of data blocks [S1]. This is a key limitation compared to DFT/DHT [S2].
+*   **Precision:** Decompression introduces quantization errors if lossy compression is used [S1].
+*   **Boundaries:** The DCT assumes even symmetry at boundaries. If the volume data does not naturally satisfy this, artifacts may occur at the edges [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The preprocessing step (3D DCT) costs $O(N^3 \log N)$ using fast algorithms, where $N$ is the volume dimension [28-domain-transform-methods.md]. The per-view rendering cost is dominated by the 2D slice extraction and inverse transform, resulting in $O(N^2)$ complexity [28-domain-transform-methods.md].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The frequency spectrum requires $O(N^3)$ storage. If block-based compression is used, memory usage is reduced, but on-demand decompression adds computational overhead [S1, S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Independent bases and fast forward/inverse transforms make DCT practical for implementation [S2]. It maps real input values to real output values, avoiding complex number arithmetic [S2].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   **No Direct Rendering:** Requires on-demand decompression of data blocks, which can be slower than direct frequency domain rendering [S1, S2].
-    *   **No Transparency/Attenuation:** Produces order-independent linear projections and does not support alpha blending or transparency control [28-domain-transform-methods.md].
-    *   **Fixed Parameters:** Interactive modification of transfer functions, lighting, or shading models is not supported because these operations are non-linear [28-domain-transform-methods.md].
-    *   **Aliasing:** Requires careful handling of the Nyquist criterion to avoid aliasing artifacts [28-domain-transform-methods.md].
-*   **When to Use:** When fast forward/inverse transforms and real-valued arithmetic are prioritized, and when the volume can be pre-compressed with block-based DCT [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When direct rendering from the compressed domain, transparency, or dynamic transfer functions are required [S1, S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The global transformation is $O(N^3 \log N)$ using fast DCT algorithms [S2]. The per-frame rendering cost is dominated by the on-demand decompression and interpolation of intersecting blocks, which is generally higher than DFT/DHT methods that can resample directly from the compressed spectrum [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed coefficient array requires significantly less memory than the raw volume, depending on the compression ratio [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Independent bases, fast forward/inverse transforms, real-to-real mapping [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot render directly from the compressed domain; requires decompression overhead [S1, S2]. Does not allow direct application of non-trivial shading models, transfer functions, and perspective projections in the compressed domain [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When memory bandwidth is a bottleneck and the scene allows for block-based processing, or when the fast DCT algorithms are preferred over DFT [S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and the overhead of decompression is unacceptable, or when complex shading models are required [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DCT-based Volume Rendering was developed by Yeo et al. [YL95] as a strategy to decompress blocks of data on demand for frequency-domain rendering [S2]. The Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) is a frequency domain transform using only cosine waves as basis representation [S2]. It is often chosen in practice for its independent bases and fast forward/inverse transforms [S2]. Unlike the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) and Discrete Hartley Transform (DHT), which support direct rendering from the compression domain, DCT does not [S2].
+DCT-based Volume Rendering was developed by Yeo et al. [YL95] as a strategy to decompress blocks of data on demand for frequency-domain rendering [S2]. It is a simplified alternative to DFT since it uses only cosine waves as a basis representation [S2]. It is often chosen in practice since it offers independent bases as well as a fast forward and inverse transforms [S2].
 
